   219	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   220	
   221	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   222	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   223	
   224	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   225	
   226	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting, answering
   227	  only what is decidable here and now — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, or
   228	  `transport-error` for a handler that fails locally; see *Settled before
   229	  Phase 1*. The handle is writer+closable only; `append!` with transient
   230	  `full` (including while the connection is still establishing),
   231	  `invalid-value` for unencodable values, `closed` once down; `close!`
   232	  means disconnect, reattachable;
   233	  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.
   234	- **Server side**: the accepted-connection handle — writer+closable, minted
   235	  from a socket the runtime handed over, so neither `create!` nor `attach!`
   236	  produces it — with the same `full`, `closed`, `transport-error`,
   237	  send-racing-close, and identity rules as the client handle; plus that
   238	  connection's own boundary adapter for its inbound events.
   239	- **Transport constructor**: the entry functions a host puts in its dispatch
   240	  table are closures this constructor produces, having received the deposit
   241	  destination and its admission declaration. Bare namespace vars could reach
   242	  that state only through namespace globals.
   243	- **Handshake**: encode and recognize the served-stream presentation and the
   244	  authoritative disclaimer as settled at the wire-contract gate and recorded
   245	  in `dao.stream.ws.md` — wire protocol, so
   246	  both ends of it are transport, not composition.
   247	- **Boundary adapter**: deposits every inbound event — payload and
   248	  lifecycle, including how the connection resolved, judging nothing — into the
   249	  host-composed deposit destination — one medium per boundary in this slice,
   250	  the granularity being composition policy per `dao.stream.ws.md` — as
   251	  `:ws/attachment`-tagged envelopes — the same value `attach!` returns under
   252	  `:dao.stream/attachment`, per `dao.stream.ws.md` — enforcing admission at assembly
   253	  against the declaration passed alongside the destination, and tearing the
   254	  connection down on any non-`ok` deposit result.
   255	- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/
   256	  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.
   257	- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never
   258	  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the
   259	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   260	  served stream), wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A
   261	  transport requiring another transport is the legacy registry defect
   262	  reborn.
   263	
   264	### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)
   265	
   266	Serving is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the
   267	transport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under
   268	the contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through
   269	its own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing
   270	drives it from below — a driver does, by calling it.
   271	
   272	That transfer is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and
   273	appends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring
   274	buffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that
   275	it belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a
   276	third layer between them.
   277	
   278	The step-versus-loop question is settled: the deliverable is a **single
   279	step**, `forward-step`, not a self-rescheduling loop. A loop that hands
   280	itself to a scheduler is a relocated callback — a continuation handed to
   281	host machinery to invoke later — and it needs a cancellation mechanism
   282	nothing here names. A step needs neither: a driver calling a function
   283	repeatedly is ordinary control flow. `forward-step` consumes the source
   284	handle, the destination handle, its state — the cursor to read from — and
   285	the two policies below; it drains at most the batch budget and returns its
   286	next state: the cursor to continue from, plus a status saying either that it
   287	should be called again (progress made, or a retryable `blocked` or `full`)
   288	or that it is finished, with the terminal condition carried as data.
   289	
   290	`forward-step` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and
   291	are the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:
   292	
   293	- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and
   294	  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;
   295	- `blocked` returns to the driver rather than spinning — on a cooperative
   296	  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains
   297	  and `full` never clears; returning is the yield;
   298	- `end` returns the terminal state `source-ended` and closes nothing.
   299	  `forward-step` is transport-agnostic, so it cannot select the close code
   300	  `dao.stream.ws.md` requires for an ended served stream — a generic
   301	  `close!` is a plain disconnect, and the client's fast path would silently
   302	  degrade to reattach-and-be-disclaimed. The transport-specific close
   303	  belongs to the composition that reads the state and knows its transport
   304	  (4c).
   305	
   306	It owns nothing else. Two policies are arguments, because each has different
   307	right answers in different compositions:
   308	
   309	- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or terminate. A
   310	  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped
   311	  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;
   312	- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before returning, which
   313	  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding.
   314	
   315	Everything else the loop form would have owned belongs to the **driver**,
   316	the composition-supplied code that calls `forward-step`. Cadence is the
   317	driver's — "run this again later" is `setTimeout` on Node, a sleeping thread
   318	on the JVM, and the concern of the runtime driving the interpreter, never of
   319	the stream (contract, *The readiness extension*). Starting is the driver's
   320	first call; nothing self-installs. Stopping is the driver ceasing to call it
   321	while keeping the returned state — which is also the cancellation mechanism —
   322	and restarting is calling it again with that state. The kept state's cursor
   323	never moved, which is what makes flow control (4d) need no protocol for
   324	saying where to continue: the sender never lost its place, so a paused
   325	connection resumes without being told anything.
   326	
   327	`forward-step` is written against the contract rather than against a named
   328	transport, so it is the one interpreter that must be **total over the outcome
   329	algebra** — every outcome of `next` and of `append!`, including those its
   330	particular endpoints never produce. Beyond the disciplines above that means an
   331	explicit branch for destination `closed`, `invalid-value`, and
   332	`transport-error`, and for source `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` (its
   333	start cursor is caller-supplied and may not belong to the source), and
   334	`transport-error`. These return a terminal state carrying the outcome rather
   335	than retrying: only `full` and `blocked` are the retryable answers. Otherwise
   336	the contract's totality is bought by moving partiality into its first
   337	consumer.
   338	
   339	Testable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time
   340	after Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.
   341	
   342	### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)
   343	
   344	What is left once the step is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only
   345	the host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must
   346	define:
   347	
   348	- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;
   349	- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;
   350	- which identities this endpoint serves — the resolution table's contents.
   351	  The handshake's wire vocabulary is not composition; it belongs to 4a;
   352	- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how
   353	  that identity reaches deposited events;
   354	- one forwarder per connection — a driver stepping `forward-step` from the
   355	  served stream to that connection's ws handle — with the cadence, batch
   356	  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;
   357	- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served
   358	  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges
   359	  nothing;
   360	- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does
   361	  to connected attachments. When a connection's forwarder returns
   362	  `source-ended`, this composition performs the transport-specific close —
   363	  the ended-stream close code per `dao.stream.ws.md`, settled at the
   364	  wire-contract gate — which `forward-step` deliberately cannot (see 4b).
   365	
   366	### 4d — Flow control
   367	
   368	Without this, a reader that falls behind loses data: the deposit medium evicts
   369	its oldest values and the reader is told it missed them. That is honest, and
   370	right for a live feed where the newest value is the one that matters. It is
   371	wrong as the only option, and it makes eviction the normal mode rather than
   372	the backstop it should be.
   373	
   374	Nothing here changes the ws transport or the contract; the one piece of
   375	mechanism, the measurement, is a ring buffer transport-owned operation
   376	delivered in Phase 2. Pause requests travel as ordinary values
   377	on the socket the client already writes to, and the serving side reads them
   378	off its own request medium (see `dao.stream.ws.md`, *Serving*). Two
   379	interpreters, one per end.
   380	
   381	- **The reader publishes its own backlog.** Only it can: a stream holds no
   382	  reader positions, so neither the medium nor the adapter can know how far
   383	  behind anyone is. Occupancy relative to *your* cursor is private to you.
   384	  It measures that occupancy with the deposit medium's transport-owned `lag`
   385	  operation (Phase 2), handed to it by the same composition that supplies
   386	  its threshold; the contract's public surface gains no operation for this.
   387	  When its lag passes the threshold its composition configured, the reader
   388	  asks for a pause.
   389	
   390	  A `gap` on the measured medium outranks any measurement: the reader
   391	  recovers its cursor and measures again before requesting or renewing
   392	  anything. An evicted cursor is maximally behind by definition, so without
   393	  this rule the reader renews a pause forever against a position that no
   394	  longer exists — pausing harder in exactly the situation that calls for gap
   395	  recovery instead.
   396	- **A pause is a lease, not a switch.** The request carries a duration — hold
   397	  for *n* — and the reader renews while it is still behind. A switch has a
   398	  stuck state: a lost resume, or a reader that dies mid-pause, leaves the
   399	  sender stopped forever. A lease expires, so absent renewal the system
   400	  returns to sending on its own, and a dead reader is indistinguishable from
   401	  one that caught up.
   402	- **The serving composition acts on it**: stop stepping the connection's
   403	  forwarder, resume stepping when the lease lapses or the reader stops
   404	  renewing. The kept state's cursor never moved, so nothing says where to
   405	  resume.
   406	- **Liveness**: the serving side pings on an interval and drops a connection
   407	  after two unanswered. A connection can die without either side being told,
   408	  and a paused forwarder would otherwise sit indefinitely on a peer that is
   409	  gone.
   410	- **Caps**: a maximum lease duration, so one request cannot buy an hour, and a
   411	  maximum consecutive paused time, after which the connection is dropped
   412	  rather than renewed forever.
   413	
   414	**Why this cannot be turned against the server.** The served stream evicts on
   415	its own schedule regardless of who is attached, so a client that pauses and
   416	never returns causes the server to retain nothing extra — it simply falls
   417	behind and comes back to a `gap`. The loss moves upstream, bounded by the
   418	served stream's retention, and lands where the producer can be told about it.
   419	A client that pauses forever harms only itself. The caps above make that
   420	bounded rather than merely self-correcting.
   421	
   422	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   423	
   424	Two processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is
   425	worth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor
   426	is self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity
   427	across — and those are central contract promises.
   428	
   429	Process A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B
   430	receives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host
   431	boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B
   432	reads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:
   433	how B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.
   434	
   435	Kill the connection, then verify four separable facts:
   436	
   437	1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,
   438	   A's boundary deposits the departure.
   439	2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor
   440	   rejoins the same logical stream.
   441	3. Two simultaneous attachments to the same stream carry distinct
   442	   `:dao.stream/attachment` values in their `attach!` results, and every event
   443	   deposited for one — payload, resolution, and lifecycle alike — carries that
   444	   same value under `:ws/attachment`.
   445	4. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium
   446	   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used
   447	   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level
   448	   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in
   449	   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.
   450	
   451	## Host matrix
   452	
   453	- **Phases 1–3** are pure `.cljc` with no transport: they must compile and
   454	  pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Nothing in them is host-specific, and
   455	  a parity break here is cheapest to find here.
   456	- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is
   457	  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and
   458	  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the
   459	  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.
   460	- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — `forward-step` takes no scheduler
   461	  and has no host dependency at all; cadence lives in the driver — so it
   462	  compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer tests pass on all three hosts,
   463	  including where the ws transport does not yet exist.
   464	- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,
   465	  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec
   466	  produces structurally identical values across all three.
   467	
   468	## What v1 callers lose, and what replaces it
   469	
   470	The contract is a clean-room redesign: `dao.stream.md` neither mentions v1 nor
   471	owes it compatibility, and nothing in `dao.stream` may consult this table.
   472	It lives here because this plan is the migration, and because the 52 `open!`
   473	call sites need to know what happened to the call they were making. It is
   474	consumed with the rest of this document.
   475	
   476	| v1 (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)     | v2                            | Why                                                                                       |
   477	|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   478	| `IDaoStreamReader` — `next`         | reader — `cursor`, `next`     | Cursors are minted by the stream instead of built inline as `{:position 0}`, so a transport owns its cursor representation and `cursor-mismatch` becomes detectable. |
   479	| `IDaoStreamWriter` — `append!`      | writer — `append!`            | Unchanged in shape; the outcome set is now exhaustive and `append!` never throws on a closed stream — it answers `closed`. |
   480	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `close!`        | closable — `close!`           | Outcome set is `{ok}`, idempotent.                                                        |
   481	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `closed?`       | **gone**                      | A predicate answer is stale the moment it returns; operate the handle and read the outcome. |
   482	| `IDaoStreamWaitable` — 2 fns        | **gone**                      | No readiness extension. A `blocked` reader retries when it chooses; cadence belongs to the runtime driving it. |
   483	| `IDaoStreamDrainable` / `drain-one!` | **gone**                     | Destructive read makes one reader's progress every other reader's data loss.               |
   484	| `open!` multimethod, `defopen` macro | **gone**                     | Ambient namespace-global registry with load-time registration. Replaced by an ordinary host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to `{create attach}`, looked up by the host. |
   485	| `open!` on a descriptor              | `create!` or `attach!`        | v1 conflated them. Creation takes a creation specification; attachment takes a portable descriptor and never creates. |
   486	| `take!!`                             | **gone**                      | Blocking. JVM-only by construction, and unimplementable on cljs/cljd.                      |
   487	| `strict-vec`                         | **gone**                      | Throws on ordinary operational signals; those are outcomes.                                |
   488	| `->seq`                              | **gone**                      | An interpreter over a stream, not part of the contract (see the contract's Composition).   |
   489	| bare `:ok` / `:blocked` / `:end` / `:daostream/gap` returns | outcome maps under `:dao.stream/…` | Every operation returns an open map keyed by `:dao.stream/outcome`, so extensions add keys without breaking consumers. |
   490	| `{:position 0}` cursors              | transport-owned cursor values | There is no `seek`; every valid cursor comes from the stream.                              |
   491	| `descriptor` via handle metadata     | `descriptor` operation        | Identity is universal, answered by every handle, and outlives any attachment.              |
   492	
   493	Migrating a call site is therefore mostly mechanical — `open!` splits, results
   494	become maps, cursors come from `cursor` — with two places that need a decision
   495	rather than a translation: anything relying on `closed?` must be restructured
   496	to act on an outcome, and anything relying on `drain-one!` or `take!!` needs a
   497	cursor and a retry policy its runtime owns.
   498	
   499	## Boundary of this plan
   500	
   501	Carried, with a term:
   502	
   503	- The slice's boundary deposits into a ring buffer rather than into the
   504	  medium ADR 0003 names, under a time-boxed exception that ADR records. The
   505	  exception ends when a conforming writer answers with data and a composition
   506	  indexes and publishes what the boundary deposits. Until then this plan is
   507	  where the exception is being spent, so it is named here and not only there.
   508	
   509	Forbidden, not deferred:
   510	
   511	- **Retention that waits for readers.** A stream that declines to evict until
   512	  every attached cursor has passed a position is one line from the design
   513	  above and has a failure mode none of the rest do: a single stalled or
   514	  departed reader holds the stream open indefinitely, and a hostile one does
   515	  it on purpose. Bounded retention must stay bounded. Loss is reported as
   516	  `gap`; it is never prevented by waiting.
   517	
   518	Not in this plan, by design:
   519	
   520	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   521	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   522	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   523	- The waiter/readiness extension, Shibi, ws resumption protocol, cursor
   524	  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the
   525	  contract, deferred here.
   526	
   527	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   528	
   529	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   530	not to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and
   531	cljs; it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport
   532	lands, and the end condition is not met before then.
   533	
   534	When the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its
   535	own plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken
   536	explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name
   537	permanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the
   538	migration, not a steady state.

