docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-381-- **The reader publishes its own backlog.** Only it can: a stream holds no
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:382:  reader positions, so neither the medium nor the adapter can know how far
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-383-  behind anyone is. Occupancy relative to *your* cursor is private to you.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-384-  It measures that occupancy with the deposit medium's transport-owned `lag`
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-385-  operation (Phase 2), handed to it by the same composition that supplies
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-386-  its threshold; the contract's public surface gains no operation for this.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-387-  When its lag passes the threshold its composition configured, the reader
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:388:  asks for a pause.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-389-
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:390:  A `gap` on the measured medium outranks any measurement: the reader
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:391:  recovers its cursor and measures again before requesting or renewing
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:392:  anything. An evicted cursor is maximally behind by definition, so without
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:393:  this rule the reader renews a pause forever against a position that no
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:394:  longer exists — pausing harder in exactly the situation that calls for gap
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-395-  recovery instead.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:396:- **A pause is a lease, not a switch.** The request carries a duration — hold
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:397:  for *n* — and the reader renews while it is still behind. A switch has a
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:398:  stuck state: a lost resume, or a reader that dies mid-pause, leaves the
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:399:  sender stopped forever. A lease expires, so absent renewal the system
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-400-  returns to sending on its own, and a dead reader is indistinguishable from
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-401-  one that caught up.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:402:- **The serving composition acts on it**: stop stepping the connection's
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:403:  forwarder, resume stepping when the lease lapses or the reader stops
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:404:  renewing. The kept state's cursor never moved, so nothing says where to
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-405-  resume.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:406:- **Liveness**: the serving side pings on an interval and drops a connection
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-407-  after two unanswered. A connection can die without either side being told,
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:408:  and a paused forwarder would otherwise sit indefinitely on a peer that is
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-409-  gone.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:410:- **Caps**: a maximum lease duration, so one request cannot buy an hour, and a
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:411:  maximum consecutive paused time, after which the connection is dropped
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:412:  rather than renewed forever.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-413-
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:414:**Why this cannot be turned against the server.** The served stream evicts on
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:415:its own schedule regardless of who is attached, so a client that pauses and
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-416-never returns causes the server to retain nothing extra — it simply falls
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:417:behind and comes back to a `gap`. The loss moves upstream, bounded by the
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-418-served stream's retention, and lands where the producer can be told about it.
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:419:A client that pauses forever harms only itself. The caps above make that
docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md-420-bounded rather than merely self-correcting.
